<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aragonese Crusade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1284">1284</a>–<a href="/years/1285">1285</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aragonese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> was a crusade declared by Pope Martin IV against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a>, who had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a> invaded Catalonia in 1285 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>, but disease and the destruction of his fleet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> forced a retreat, and Philip died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aragonese-crusade.jpg" alt="Aragonese Crusade" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setge-girona-1285.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Aragonese Crusade was fought as an extension of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a>, which lasted from 1282 to 1302. Pope Martin IV called it in retribution for the intervention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which had damaged the political ambitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Martin declared the crusade in 1284 and 1285 and formally deposed Peter, on the ground that Aragon was a papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>: Peter's grandfather and namesake, Peter II, had surrendered the kingdom to the Holy See as a fief. Martin gave Aragon to Count Charles of Valois, a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> split Aragon. Peter's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-majorca">James II of Majorca</a>, who had inherited the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and so stood between the French and Aragonese dominions, joined the French; Peter had opposed his inheritance as a younger son. In 1284 the first French armies entered Roussillon, numbering 16,000 cavalry, 17,000 crossbowmen and 100,000 infantry, with 100 ships in southern French ports. The local people rose against them. The Bastard of Roussillon defended Elne, but the city was overcome and its cathedral burned, and the population was massacred except for him.</p><p>In 1285 Philip III entrenched himself before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> and took it. On 28 April Cardinal Jean Cholet placed his own hat on Charles's head, and Charles was nicknamed the king of the hat. The French then suffered reverses. Peter's admiral Roger de Lauria destroyed their fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-les-formigues">Battle of Les Formigues</a>, near Palamós, and dysentery struck the camp, afflicting Philip himself. The heir, Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, negotiated free passage for the royal family, but the troops were refused it and were decimated at the Battle of the Col de Panissars. The king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>. Peter did not long survive him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The defeat of 1285 effectively ended the fighting. The Treaty of Tarascon of 1291 restored Aragon to Alfonso III and lifted the Church's ban. The historian H. J. Chaytor judged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> perhaps the most unjust, unnecessary and calamitous enterprise ever undertaken by the Capetian monarchy, and W. C. Jordan has blamed it for the young Philip IV's later opposition to papal interference in French foreign policy. Its legacy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was slight, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> was devastated as an independent polity: Alfonso annexed Majorca, Ibiza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a> of 1295 returned the islands to James.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aragonese Crusade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…won a series of victories against the Angevins after Peter III took Sicily in 1282: off Malta in 1283, in the Bay of Naples in 1284, where Charles of Salerno was captured, and at Les Formigues in 1285, which wrecked the French invasion known as the <b>Aragonese Crusade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The admiral Roger of Lauria won a series of victories against the Angevins and the French, and when Philip III of France invaded Catalonia in the <b>Aragonese Crusade</b> of 1285, the French army was forced to retreat after its fleet was destroyed at the Battle of Les Formigues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
